I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the reusable diffraction workflows first, then build a phase inventory and refine it in GSAS‑II with residual-led checks. I’ll preserve intermediate evidence and verify that the final GPX, CIF exports, fractions, and profile all agree.

The chemistry points first toward manganese tungstates, but I’m treating unreacted oxides and alternate Mn–W–O stoichiometries/polymorphs as live candidates. Next I’m extracting the peak families and checking the local structure inventory before any refinement, so weak or poorly matching records are not prematurely discarded.

The peak-family test now gives a coherent provisional inventory: monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ plus cubic bixbyite Mn₂O₃. A common ≈+0.29° angular offset aligns multiple isolated families of both phases (not just the intense 23–25° cluster), while MnWO₄’s diagnostic 15.5°, 18.5°, and 30.0/30.45° families are absent. I’m keeping MnWO₄ and Mn₃WO₆ on the watch list until the common-profile residual is established.

The first common-profile fit converged reproducibly to about 72.8 wt% WO₃ / 27.2 wt% Mn₂O₃, but it is not yet the final model: alternating residuals around isolated peaks show that a constant zero correction is slightly wrong for this reflection-geometry scan. I’m now comparing the physically appropriate specimen-displacement correction and alternate structural records under the same background/profile model before accepting fractions.

Refining the unknown effective polarization was strongly supported (0.744 ± 0.006) and reduced Rwp from 7.70% to 7.00% without a texture term; this confirms the cross-phase intensity trend was instrumental, not a scale/background surrogate. I’m now re-running the alternate WO₃ and Mn₂O₃ records and the phase-specific broadening trials against this corrected common profile.

One baseline defect emerged during the polarization replay: the redundant Gaussian constant term W drifted slightly negative. I’m pruning it to the physical boundary (W = 0, fixed), retaining positive U and X, and then testing the repeated low-angle asymmetry with a single SH/L term. This is the required return to the baseline rather than accepting an interior-looking but nonphysical profile.

The physical baseline now replays cleanly with W fixed at zero; SH/L was unsupported and 98% correlated with displacement, so it is pruned. The isotropic size trials either hit the neutral bound or were weakly correlated; two phase-specific strain terms replayed stably and are being carried provisionally while I test texture axes and watch-list phases.

The phase inventory is now stable: monoclinic WO₃ and cubic Mn₂O₃ dominate, with a reproducible trace MnWO₄ contribution. I’m producing the final replayed GSAS-II project and exports now, then I’ll cross-check every reported fraction and profile point against that saved project.

The final replay converged cleanly (maximum shift/e.s.d. 0.011), and the three crystalline fractions normalize to 100.000%. The trace phase refines to 0.605 wt%; I’m treating that number cautiously because its cell had to remain fixed to the source structure to prevent an unsupported drift.

One source audit caught an important nuance before handoff: the provisional MnWO₄ record comes from a high-pressure study and carries a pressure field, despite its otherwise physical 293 K structure. I’m checking ambient-pressure MnWO₄ records under the same fixed-cell nuisance model; if one fits equivalently, I’ll replace the trace CIF with the better-matched source record and replay everything.

The ambient-compatible alternate is materially better: a 4.2 K hübnerite record with only 3% Fe on the Mn site (replaced by Mn) fits better at fixed cell than the high-pressure record and gives about 0.80 wt% MnWO₄. Releasing its cell moves it to a plausible near-ambient volume and about 1.08 wt%, but I’m replaying that model further because a trace-phase cell is only acceptable if it is genuinely stable, not merely chemically plausible.

The selected trace model is now fixed and replay-stable at 0.798 wt%, with a lower Rwp than the pressure-tagged alternative. Its strongest diagnostic residuals near 18.69°, 30.22°, and 30.57° are substantially reduced. I’m doing the required final watch-list re-challenge against this rebuilt common profile before freezing the report.

The final watch-list pass leaves the three-phase inventory unchanged. Mn₃O₄ collapses to zero; Mn₃WO₆ reaches only 0.22 ± 0.11 wt% and improves a few overlaps while worsening several of its strongest predicted families, so it is not a defensible fourth phase. I’ve written the result and uncertainty report and am running the final artifact-consistency checks.

Completed the crystalline phase analysis. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Monoclinic WO₃, P2₁/c | 71.840 wt% |
| Bixbyite Mn₂O₃, Ia-3 | 27.362 wt% |
| MnWO₄, P2/c | 0.798 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 6.9088%, Rp 5.1260%, GOF 2.5986. The MnWO₄ presence is supported, but its practical fraction is model-dependent, approximately 0.6–1.1 wt%. A subtle cubic-versus-orthorhombic distortion of Mn₂O₃ cannot be resolved reliably.

